anti-discrimination, and respect for intellectual&nbsp;
property, though these rules would not constrain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:38.040
the Chinese government itself. The regulation&nbsp;
also stipulates that output from generative AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:43.920
must “reflect the core values of socialism,”&nbsp;
a constraint that could shape how their AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.460
develops. 
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:54.660
Are we going to have to rely on each country&nbsp;
to make its own regulations? I mean, is there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.660 --> 00:18:00.000
a possibility of a global regulatory body?
HAVEN: You know, there is a possibility of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.200
global regulatory body.
SIERRA: There's always a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:07.680
possibility really, yeah.
HAVEN: There have been calls from multiple places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:17.340
for an international regulatory body. That strikes&nbsp;
me as really difficult to achieve if we don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:25.020
a clear sense of our own regulatory structure, but&nbsp;
critically our own values in regulating AI.
&nbsp;

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:32.580
MALLABY: People talk about having an equivalent to&nbsp;
the IAEA, the International Atomic Energy Agency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:39.240
which tries to set rules around the use&nbsp;
of nuclear power and sort of supervise and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:46.500
prevent proliferation through inspections and so&nbsp;
forth. And I think that sort of international body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:56.220
would be a good addition to the global system.&nbsp;
I think, though, that one thing which is obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.220 --> 00:19:02.820
for anybody who studies international relations&nbsp;
is that you tend to get to results through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:11.340
combination of supranational and national and&nbsp;
private sector initiative. And no one body is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:16.680
going to be a silver bullet. So you're going&nbsp;
to need national regulation as well. And you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:23.400
going to need a lot of industries that think&nbsp;
actively about developing best practice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:29.040
self-regulate. I think the good thing here is that&nbsp;
there already is conversation going on between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:35.280
industry and governments. There's conversation&nbsp;
between companies, although they are competitors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:41.160
they also talk about safety and ethics&nbsp;
and responsible AI with each other. 
&nbsp;

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:46.620
SIERRA: Do you think that countries&nbsp;
can or should push to limit private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:51.300
development? Sort of force everyone into this&nbsp;
Manhattan Project model where everything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:56.400
done under the eye of the government?
MALLABY: I think we're just not in a place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.400 --> 00:20:03.600
it's credible to believe that the government can&nbsp;
take over all AI development. That would take such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:09.180
a radical act of nationalization and disruption.&nbsp;
I think that's probably too extreme. But I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:15.960
what you can do, and what's kind of happening&nbsp;
anyway is that there will be a huge amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:22.800
dialogue between the AI developers and people&nbsp;
in government. And people in government are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:28.200
already going to the obvious big developers,&nbsp;
Microsoft, Google, et cetera, and saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:34.260
“You know, listen, we know that when you publish&nbsp;
AI research papers, these are downloaded in vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:38.400
numbers in China. Just be a bit selective about&nbsp;
what you're publishing, a bit careful.”
&nbsp;

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:44.460
SIERRA: I've lived through a lot of the&nbsp;
emergence of the internet in my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:51.060
and I don't really recall a moment where&nbsp;
regulators or “good guys” seemed to be ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:57.900
of “bad guys” or the rogue actors. Do you&nbsp;
think that this will be the case with AI as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:03.060
well? Has it been the case with AI as well?
HAVEN: There's a lot of literature and research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:08.280
on what's called the pacing problem, which&nbsp;
is exactly what you're talking about. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:14.640
technological development tends to outpace&nbsp;
regulation, but in many ways that's less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:20.940
about the regulation of the bad guys and&nbsp;
more about the regulation of the guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:33.900
of the companies who have the greatest access to&nbsp;
data, compute, talent, and money, and who are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:40.080
you know, falling outside of regulatory controls.&nbsp;
So I would back up and say I think that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:47.220
a pacing problem with, you know, regulating&nbsp;
theoretically well-intentioned companies or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:55.860
you know, capitalist markets. That said, we&nbsp;
know that humans will use technical systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.860 --> 00:22:02.640
in all kinds of surprising ways, both,&nbsp;
you know, wonderful and entertaining and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:09.960
also nefarious and adversarial. And so, I&nbsp;
think regulating with the idea that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:17.820
only regulating the good actors or the sort of&nbsp;
known actors and not considering the adversarial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:23.820
uses of AI would be a huge mistake. 
SIERRA: One major concern that people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:29.580
is that very soon we won't know if a piece of&nbsp;
content: writing, news, music, et cetera, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:35.220
created by AI. Do you think there should be a rule&nbsp;
that AI always has to tell us that it's AI?
&nbsp;

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:41.640
MALLABY: Yes. I think there's a question about&nbsp;
how you enforce the rule if some of the AI’s are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:48.780
based in Russia or wherever. But here again, I&nbsp;
think the optimistic vision is that, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:56.340
the responsible players in AI are going to include&nbsp;
what they call watermarks, either in the music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.340 --> 00:23:02.700
that is generated, like computer generated music,&nbsp;
computer generated images, computer generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:10.500
text, all of these things will have signatures.&nbsp;
So you, as a normal internet surfer, could see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:17.460
great picture on some internet platform and then&nbsp;
click, you know, like an information button and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:22.620
say who generated this? Was it a human? Was it&nbsp;
a machine? And if it's a machine, the machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:29.220
would reveal its identity to you. I think that's&nbsp;
a super useful protection against deep fakes. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:34.140
risk is of course, that responsible creators&nbsp;
of AI will do that, and irresponsible people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:38.160
will be offshore and they won't, and the bad&nbsp;
actors will use the irresponsible versions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:42.720
AI. So then comes the question, what do you&nbsp;
do about that? I think this is all something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:47.340
that's being discussed and worked out in real&nbsp;
time, but those are some of the indications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:52.440
of where we might find a solution.
SIERRA: There’s just so much to consider when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:58.920
we look to the future of AI. And while the United&nbsp;
States may be ahead on the development, we’re way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:05.280
behind on the regulation. The rules and guidelines&nbsp;
we establish today are likely to have profound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:12.600
impacts on the future of society. Getting AI right&nbsp;
could be a very good thing, and getting it wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:18.900
could be a very bad thing indeed. Almost everyone&nbsp;
on earth has an interest in getting it right.
&nbsp;

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:26.460
SIERRA: What makes a responsible AI? 
MALLABY: Part of it is designing it in such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:34.440
a way that humans feel agency and feel that&nbsp;
they can understand what the AI is doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:42.960
There's a whole field of trying to think of ways&nbsp;
to reduce the black box element of artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:49.980
intelligence. So explainability is improved. So&nbsp;
when you’re given an output, you might be given A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:55.440
a confidence level around how confident&nbsp;
the algorithm is that it got that right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.220 --> 00:25:02.400
to B, maybe some indication of how it got to that&nbsp;
answer. There's a whole field now called alignment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:06.960
within consumer computer science, which is to&nbsp;
align the machines with what humans want from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.960 --> 00:25:13.500
machines. And again, this is a sort of thing which&nbsp;
gives me some optimism that the people building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:20.160
this stuff care about making it good.
HAVEN: I think that there is a very strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:31.080
desire to see a check on what feels like a runaway&nbsp;
industry that has amassed an enormous amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:39.360
power and money and decision-making ability to&nbsp;
set the agenda on a technology that will impact us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:48.000
all. And I think that we need to think about the&nbsp;
AI moment in that way, not as a moment of clamping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:54.000
down and, you know, shrinking opportunity, but as&nbsp;
actually a moment of hugely increased opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:02.280
to think about the ways in which we can use a&nbsp;
technology like this to mitigate climate change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:11.160
to find new energy sources, to solve seemingly&nbsp;
intractable health problems. And I think that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:19.740
entirely possible, but I also think that it&nbsp;
requires societal investment and agency and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:22.960
shaping that we don't have right now. 
